    "body": "Evos raised $9 million to expand AI software that audits freight invoices, builds loads and follows up on paperwork for small and mid-sized businesses. The New York startup announced the funding Oct. 14 and said its software learns how employees make decisions before doing the work on its own.\nWhat’s Related\nUncork Capital and Field Ventures co-led the round, with participation from Crosslink Capital, Utopia Capital Management, Sandwith Ventures, and angel investors. Evos said the money will fund its growth and the launch of Workhorse, its platform for industrial businesses.\nThe software is designed for freight , third-party logistics , manufacturing , and other businesses. Tasks include auditing carrier invoices, building loads, following up on paperwork and checking information against a company’s transportation management system .\nEvos said it passed $1 million in annual recurring revenue four weeks after going live. That figure reflects annualized recurring revenue, not revenue collected during those four weeks.\nLearning the job first\nEvos calls its software tools “autonomous operators.” The company says they learn from a team’s behavior, including its decisions and the patterns behind them, and work within the business software a customer already uses.\n“The most capable system in many of these businesses is the person who has been there 20 years, and what they know has never been written down,” said Urav Shah, founder and CEO of Evos. “An Evos operator learns the job from these people, improves on it, and proves it can match them before it takes on any work by itself.”\nAt the start, the company says every decision goes to the customer’s team for approval. A task runs unattended only after the software’s decisions consistently match the team’s, while unfamiliar situations go back to a person.\nEach customer receives a model trained only on its own data , Evos said. The software works on top of existing systems without requiring a migration, and the company says customers can go from discovery to deployment in about 24 hours.\nA freight invoice audit\nAt one freight brokerage, an Evos operator checks less-than-truckload carrier invoices against the company’s TMS. Evos said the operator made nearly 3,500 audit decisions over two months.\nAccording to the company, human managers accepted 97.3% of those decisions exactly as proposed. That figure measures how often reviewers agreed with the software’s decisions.\nThe operator also closed out more than $600,000 in invoice value over five weeks, according to Evos. The company did not report how much the audit saved or recovered in overcharges.",
